    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d332">

                <pb facs="0443" n="341" sameAs="frus:frus1919v01/pg341"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.00/3093</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Consul General at Shanghai</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Sammons</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Minister in China</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Reinsch</hi>)<anchor n="45"
                        xml:id="d332-f1"/></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Shanghai</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1919-04-07"><hi rendition="#italic">April 7,
                        1919</hi></date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 2939</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to report the resumption
                    today of the Internal Peace Conference. While Tong Shao-yi, the chief of the <pb
                        facs="0444" n="342" xml:id="pg342"/> Southern Delegation, is not entirely
                    satisfied with reference to the cessation of hostilities in Shensi Province, he
                    has decided to join in the holding of informal, secret meetings. It would seem
                    that both the Northern and the Southern Delegations feel that the continuance of
                    the publicity feature of the conference would, at this time, tend to prejudice
                    the object which it is desired should be obtained.</p>
                <p>In the meantime, the <hi rendition="#italic">Shanghai Times</hi>, (accepted as
                    being pro-Japanese in its policies), in particular, continues editorially to
                    question the sincerity of Tong Shao-yi as the leader of the Southern Delegation.
                    This newspaper procedure on the part of the <hi rendition="#italic">Shanghai
                        Times</hi>, in which it is supported, at times, by the <hi
                        rendition="#italic">North China Daily News</hi> (British), seems to be
                    calculated to foment misunderstanding between the Northern and the Southern
                    Delegations.</p>
                <p>I have [etc.]</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Thomas Sammons</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
